Technical Analysis

The vulnerability consists of two separate components.

1. Prototype Pollution

The application allows properties to be injected into Object.prototype through:

__proto__[property]=value

In this case:

__proto__[transport_url]

effectively results in control over:

Object.prototype.transport_url
2. DOM XSS Gadget

The vulnerable JavaScript performs:

if(config.transport_url) {
    let script = document.createElement('script');
    script.src = config.transport_url;
    document.body.appendChild(script);
}

Because JavaScript searches inherited properties, the following:

config.transport_url

can resolve to:

Object.prototype.transport_url

The attacker-controlled value therefore reaches:

script.src

This converts the prototype pollution vulnerability into a DOM XSS vulnerability.

Root Cause

The vulnerability is caused by the combination of:

Unsafe processing of user-controlled parameters.
Ability to modify Object.prototype.
Reliance on inherited properties.
Use of transport_url as the source of a dynamically created <script> element.
Lack of strict validation of the value assigned to script.src.

The application implicitly trusts a property that can be introduced through the JavaScript prototype chain.

Impact

An attacker exploiting this vulnerability could potentially:

Execute arbitrary JavaScript.
Perform DOM-based XSS attacks.
Manipulate JavaScript objects used by application components.
Modify application behavior.
Access information available to JavaScript in the affected origin.
Perform actions on behalf of the victim.

The exact impact depends on the gadgets available within the application.

Mitigations
Prevent Prototype Pollution

User-controlled objects should not be processed in a way that allows modification of:

__proto__
constructor
prototype
Use Objects Without a Prototype When Appropriate

For suitable data structures, applications can use:

Object.create(null)

This prevents inheritance from:

Object.prototype
Validate Object Properties

When a property must exist directly on an object, use:

Object.hasOwn(config, "transport_url")

rather than relying solely on:

if (config.transport_url)

This prevents inherited properties from being unintentionally trusted.

Validate script.src

Values controlled by users should never be assigned directly to:

script.src

without strict validation.

A whitelist of trusted script origins should be used where dynamic scripts are required.

Restrict data: Script Sources

Applications should avoid allowing:

data:

as a script source unless it is explicitly required.

Content Security Policy

A restrictive Content Security Policy can reduce the impact of this type of vulnerability.

For example:

Content-Security-Policy: script-src 'self';

This restricts script execution to approved sources.

The exact CSP should be adapted to the application's legitimate requirements.

