Conclusion

This lab demonstrates how a client-side prototype pollution vulnerability can be escalated into DOM XSS when an exploitable gadget is present.

The application allowed the attacker to pollute:

Object.prototype

through:

__proto__[transport_url]

The polluted property was then inherited by the config object:

config.transport_url

and subsequently assigned to:

script.src

Finally, the attacker supplied:

data:,alert(1);

as the value of the polluted property, resulting in arbitrary JavaScript execution.

The key lesson is that prototype pollution alone does not necessarily lead to code execution. An exploitable gadget is required to consume the polluted property in a dangerous way.

In this case:

Prototype Pollution
        +
DOM XSS Gadget
        =
Arbitrary JavaScript Execution
Key Takeaways
Properties inherited from Object.prototype can affect otherwise unrelated objects.
__proto__, constructor, and prototype should be treated as security-sensitive properties.
Identifying a prototype pollution source is only the first step.
Exploitation requires finding a suitable gadget.
Dynamic assignment to script.src is particularly dangerous when the value is attacker-controlled.
Object.hasOwn() can prevent applications from unintentionally trusting inherited properties.
Restrictive CSP policies can reduce the impact of DOM XSS gadgets.
Prototype Pollution + an exploitable gadget can result in arbitrary JavaScript execution.
